<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Hardened-runtime entitlements for DIRECT DISTRIBUTION via Developer ID.
       NOT for Mac App Store (MAS requires sandbox + provisioning profile).

       Notarisation requires hardened runtime, which blocks JIT, unsigned-
       memory execution, and loading unsigned dylibs by default. Liquid
       Clips desktop-2 is a Tauri shell with NO Python sidecar today, so
       the only relaxation we keep is the WebView V8 JIT.

       If/when a Python sidecar lands (numpy / faster-whisper / opencv),
       uncomment the two commented-out flags below — they are ported
       verbatim from the legacy desktop's entitlements-direct.plist. -->

  <!-- WKWebView V8 + WebAssembly JIT. Tauri's bundled WebView usually
       inherits system rules, but enabling explicitly is safer + faster. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- 2026-07-16 · Python sidecar landed (v0.7.64 · PyInstaller-onedir
       CPython 3.12 + faster-whisper + ctranslate2 + opencv + yt-dlp).
       The two flags below are now REQUIRED, ported verbatim from the
       legacy desktop-1 entitlements. -->

  <!-- 2026-06-25 · Runtime Update v1 · Phase 1.
       Rust code in `src/runtime.rs` uses reqwest to GET the runtime
       manifest + download bundle tarballs. Hardened runtime silently
       blocks main-process outbound network without this entitlement
       (WKWebView has its own sandboxed network process so the iframe
       worked; reqwest in the main process did not). -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- 2026-07-16 · Required by the embedded CPython interpreter.
       CPython's bytecode dispatch (ceval.c) and marshal.loads both
       write to executable memory; hardened runtime blocks that by
       default. ctranslate2's INT8 kernels and faster-whisper's
       CPU-inference path also JIT-generate ops. Without this flag
       the sidecar spawns but exits the moment the first `import`
       triggers PyImport_ImportModule -> bytecode load.
       Scope: process-local · only affects code inside Liquid Clips.app.
       Notary: standard Developer ID entitlement, no extra profile. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- 2026-07-16 · Required by Python.framework + ~195 embedded
       .so/.dylib files under _internal/. Every one is signed by our
       Developer ID (Team KT68NGT4LX) via CI's Mach-O walk. But
       hardened runtime's default library validation ALSO checks that
       every loaded library's Team ID matches the LOADING PROCESS's
       Team ID at every dlopen boundary — and CPython's internal
       PyImport_ImportModule dlopen chain fails that check for
       framework-internal loads even when both sides carry the SAME
       Team ID (documented Apple behavior). This entitlement relaxes
       that specific check WITHOUT weakening Gatekeeper's outer
       signature verification and WITHOUT allowing loads from
       arbitrary untrusted paths — libraries must still live inside
       the app bundle.
       Scope: process-local. Notary: standard Developer ID entitlement. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

</dict>
</plist>
